---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/wallpaper';
import { wallpaperRolls } from '../lib/finish';

const rooms: [number, number][] = [[10, 10], [10, 12], [12, 12], [12, 14], [14, 16], [16, 20]];
const table = rooms.map(([l, w]) => ({
  l,
  w,
  plain: wallpaperRolls({ perimeterFt: 2 * (l + w), heightFt: 8, rollWidthIn: 20.5, rollLengthFt: 33, repeatIn: 0, doors: 1 }).rolls,
  rep: wallpaperRolls({ perimeterFt: 2 * (l + w), heightFt: 8, rollWidthIn: 20.5, rollLengthFt: 33, repeatIn: 21, doors: 1 }).rolls,
}));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="wallpaper-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Wallpaper Calculator: How Many Rolls You Need, with Pattern Repeat"
  description="Free wallpaper calculator. Enter your room size, roll size, and pattern repeat to get the number of strips and rolls, counted strip by strip the way installers do."
  h1="Wallpaper Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find how many rolls of wallpaper to buy. The calculator counts full-height strips, the way installers do, and allows
    for pattern repeat and trimming.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate wallpaper rolls</h2>
  <p>
    Dividing wall area by roll area underestimates, because every strip wastes its offcut and patterned paper wastes more
    to line up the design. Count strips instead:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Strips needed:</strong> room perimeter in inches ÷ roll width, rounded up. Subtract strips for full-height openings like doors.</li>
    <li><strong>Cut length:</strong> wall height + pattern repeat + about 4 inches for trimming top and bottom.</li>
    <li><strong>Strips per roll:</strong> roll length ÷ cut length, rounded down.</li>
    <li><strong>Rolls:</strong> strips needed ÷ strips per roll, rounded up.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 12 × 12 ft room with 8 ft walls</h2>
  <p>
    The perimeter is 48 feet, or 576 inches. With 20.5-inch paper that is 28.1, so 29 strips, minus one for the door:
    28. Each strip is cut 96 + 4 = 100 inches, and a 33-foot roll (396 inches) yields 3 strips. 28 ÷ 3 = 9.3, so buy
    <strong>10 rolls</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Rolls for common room sizes</h2>
  <p>8 ft walls, 20.5 in × 33 ft rolls, one door.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Room</th><th class="num">Plain paper</th><th class="num">21 in pattern repeat</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{table.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.l} × {r.w} ft</td><td class="num">{r.plain}</td><td class="num">{r.rep}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="repeat">What is pattern repeat?</h2>
  <p>
    It is the vertical distance before the design starts over, printed on the roll label. Each strip must start at the
    same point in the pattern, so you lose up to one repeat per strip. Large repeats can drop a roll from four strips to
    three, which is why patterned paper often needs a roll or two more than plain.
  </p>

  <p>
    Papering only an accent wall or around sloped ceilings? <a href="/square-footage-calculator/">Measure the wall area</a> and compare it with
    the usable area on the roll label.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How much does a roll of wallpaper cover?</h3>
  <p>
    A standard US double roll of 20.5 inches by 33 feet is about 56 square feet, but only about 45–50 square feet are
    usable after trimming, and less with a large repeat. Check the label: many papers are sold priced per single roll but
    packaged as double rolls.
  </p>
  <h3>Why aren't windows subtracted?</h3>
  <p>
    You still paper above and below a window, and those short pieces rarely come from scraps. Leaving windows in keeps
    the estimate safe. Doors are full height, so their strips are subtracted.
  </p>
  <p>Patching walls before papering? The <a href="/drywall-calculator/">drywall calculator</a> covers new sheets.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Strips = perimeter ÷ roll width, rounded up, minus the full strips covered by 36-inch doors.</li>
    <li>Cut length = wall height + pattern repeat + 4 inches; strips per roll are rounded down.</li>
    <li>Rolls = strips ÷ strips per roll, rounded up.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
